{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/cvm-updates/fda-grants-full-approval-drug-control-seizures-dogs-idiopathic-epilepsy","https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/17867","https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/unapproved-animal-drugs/how-can-i-use-fda-online-label-repository-tell-if-drug-legally-marketed"],"proposed_value":"Not applicable/no FDA human antiseizure boxed-warning verification: no FDA-approved human potassium bromide antiseizure label was identified; FDA has approved veterinary KBroVet (potassium bromide chewable tablets) for dogs. Do not use DailyMed for this field.","rationale":"The current source is DailyMed, which is disallowed by the audit rule for boxed-warning verification. FDA sources support veterinary approval for dogs and the FDA FOI summary states the product is not for human use; they do not support a human FDA boxed-warning claim for potassium bromide.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"potassium bromide","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"potassium bromide","rationale":"Supported by German Fachinformation, PubChem/NCBI synonym record, and FDA veterinary KBroVet sources.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf","https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Potassium-Bromide","https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/17867"]},{"current_value":"bromide; kaliumbromid","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"kaliumbromid; KBr; kalii bromidum; kali bromatum","rationale":"Kaliumbromid, KBr, kalii bromidum, and kali bromatum are supported synonyms. 'bromide' is broader than potassium bromide and is better kept only as a search alias.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Potassium-Bromide"]},{"current_value":"DIBRO-BE mono; Kaliumbromid DESITIN","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"DIBRO-BE mono; Kaliumbromid DESITIN; KBroVet; KBroVet-CA1 (veterinary, US)","rationale":"Kaliumbromid DESITIN is verified from the German Fachinformation. FDA sources verify KBroVet/KBroVet-CA1 as potassium bromide chewable tablets for dogs. DIBRO-BE mono appears plausible but lacks a trusted named source in the current row.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/cvm-updates/fda-grants-full-approval-drug-control-seizures-dogs-idiopathic-epilepsy","https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/17867"]},{"current_value":"kaliumbromid; bromide; DIBRO-BE mono; Kaliumbromid DESITIN","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"potassium bromide; KBr; kaliumbromid; bromide; DIBRO-BE mono; Kaliumbromid DESITIN; KBroVet; KBroVet-CA1","rationale":"Add exact chemical abbreviation and newly verified US veterinary brand aliases; retain broad 'bromide' only for search sensitivity.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Potassium-Bromide","https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/cvm-updates/fda-grants-full-approval-drug-control-seizures-dogs-idiopathic-epilepsy"]},{"current_value":"No - not available in U.S. for seizure control; German nationally authorized product information identified.","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"No for FDA-approved human seizure control; however, FDA granted full approval to veterinary KBroVet (potassium bromide chewable tablets) for control of seizures associated with idiopathic epilepsy in dogs on January 21, 2026. German nationally authorized human product information is identified.","rationale":"The current wording is too broad after FDA's 2026 full approval of KBroVet for dogs. Human-vs-veterinary scope should be explicit.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/cvm-updates/fda-grants-full-approval-drug-control-seizures-dogs-idiopathic-epilepsy","https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/17867","https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Tablet; dispersible/splittable oral tablet","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet; splittable/dispersible oral tablet; chewable tablet (US veterinary KBroVet, if veterinary formulations are in scope)","rationale":"German Fachinformation supports scored/splittable tablets that can disintegrate in warm water or tea. FDA FOI supports 250 mg and 500 mg chewable veterinary tablets.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf","https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/17867"]},{"current_value":"Generalized tonic-clonic; Dravet; Myoclonic syndromes; Pediatric severe epilepsy","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Primary/secondary generalized tonic-clonic seizures in early-childhood grand mal epilepsy; severe childhood myoclonic syndromes including Dravet syndrome; pediatric severe/refractory epilepsy","rationale":"German Fachinformation supports generalized tonic-clonic seizures and severe childhood myoclonic syndromes such as Dravet, but also says it is not effective for myoclonic seizures themselves. PubMed retrospective data support Dravet and pediatric refractory epilepsy use but are not RCTs.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31111774/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22430156/"]},{"current_value":"Dermatologic: bromacne about 25%; CNS: fatigue rare at low bromide levels and more common with higher levels (1-10% category); GI: coated tongue/mouth odor/aphthae/constipation/diarrhea rare (0.01-0.1% category); GI: gastritis/ulcer/pancreatitis very rare (<0.01% category); respiratory: rhinitis/bronchitis/sinusitis frequency not estimable (0% exact percentage unavailable).","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"Dermatologic: papulopustular skin changes/bromacne very common, about 25%; CNS: fatigue and psychomotor slowing are serum-level related, with fatigue rare at low bromide levels and more frequent at middle/high levels, but exact percentages are not provided; GI: coated tongue, mouth odor, aphthae, constipation, or diarrhea rare (>=0.01% to <0.1%); GI: gastritis, ulcers/perforation, and pancreatitis very rare (<0.01%); Respiratory: serous rhinitis, mucus hypersecretion, bronchitis, sinusitis, and asthma exacerbation can occur, frequency not estimable from available data.","rationale":"Bromacne, GI categories, and respiratory events are supported. The CNS '1-10% category' and '0%' respiratory wording are not directly supported and should be removed.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No pharmacologic interaction with other ASMs described; not a CYP inducer/inhibitor; bromide clearance affected by chloride/salt balance and diuretics.","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"No pharmacological interactions with other antiseizure medications are described in the German Fachinformation; bromide is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged, mainly renally; bromide levels/half-life are affected by chloride/salt balance and diuretics.","rationale":"The Fachinformation supports no pharmacological interaction with other ASMs, no metabolism, renal excretion, and salt/diuretic effects. It does not specifically state CYP induction/inhibition.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"About 12 days","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"About 12 days after oral administration in humans","rationale":"Supported by the pharmacokinetic section of the German Fachinformation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No metabolism; renal excretion of bromide","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"No metabolism; bromide is excreted unchanged, almost completely renally","rationale":"Supported by the pharmacokinetic section of the German Fachinformation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"4000 mg/day maximum in German Fachinformation.","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"4000 mg/day maximum in German Fachinformation.","rationale":"The Fachinformation states total daily dose must not exceed 4000 mg.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"German labeling maintenance range begins around 40-50 mg/kg/day depending age group; individualized by serum bromide level.","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"German labeling maintenance ranges: 50-70 mg/kg/day for ages 0.5-3 years, 40-60 mg/kg/day for ages 4-15 years, and 30-50 mg/kg/day for adults continuing childhood epilepsy; individualized by serum bromide level.","rationale":"Current wording is broadly supported but imprecise; exact German dosing table is more accurate.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"German labeling: pediatric maintenance about 40-70 mg/kg/day depending age/weight; adults continuing childhood epilepsy about 30-50 mg/kg/day; divided 2-3 times daily and monitored by bromide levels.","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"German labeling: pediatric maintenance 40-70 mg/kg/day depending age/weight; adults continuing childhood epilepsy 30-50 mg/kg/day; divided into 2-3 daily doses and monitored by bromide levels.","rationale":"Supported by the German dosing table and administration/monitoring instructions.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Bromide-ion antiseizure mechanism is not fully established; German Fachinformation describes increased seizure threshold and proposed stabilization of neuronal membranes through bromide/chloride conductance with enhanced GABA-A receptor-mediated inhibitory anion current.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Bromide-ion antiseizure mechanism is not fully established; German Fachinformation describes increased seizure threshold and proposed stabilization of neuronal membranes through bromide/chloride conductance with enhanced GABA-A receptor-mediated inhibitory anion current.","rationale":"Supported by the pharmacodynamic section of the German Fachinformation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"Reasonable because the label states the exact mechanism is unknown but gives mechanistic pharmacodynamic hypotheses.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Desitin Kaliumbromid DESITIN 850 mg Fachinformation (https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf)","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"Desitin Kaliumbromid DESITIN 850 mg Fachinformation (https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf)","rationale":"The cited source supports the mechanism text, but it is a German national Fachinformation/manufacturer PDF rather than an EMA or UK SmPC source.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"EMA/UK SmPC","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"German national Fachinformation / manufacturer product information (not EMA/UK SmPC)","rationale":"The URL is a Desitin German Fachinformation PDF, not EMA or UK eMC/SmPC.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No QT interval effect described in European product information.","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No QT interval effect described in the cited German Fachinformation; no positive evidence of a clinically meaningful QT effect was identified in the cited row sources.","rationale":"The cited German product information does not describe QT/ECG effects. This supports absence of a described effect, not affirmative evidence from a dedicated QT study.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Not FDA-cleared; German Kaliumbromid DESITIN authorization 07.01.2019 in Fachinformation.","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"Not FDA-approved for human seizure control; FDA granted full approval to veterinary KBroVet (potassium bromide chewable tablets) for dogs on January 21, 2026; German Kaliumbromid DESITIN authorization date 07.01.2019.","rationale":"The German authorization date is supported. The broad 'Not FDA-cleared' statement is contradicted in the veterinary context by FDA's 2026 KBroVet full approval, so human/veterinary scope must be explicit.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/cvm-updates/fda-grants-full-approval-drug-control-seizures-dogs-idiopathic-epilepsy","https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/17867"]},{"current_value":"Desitin Kaliumbromid DESITIN 850 mg Fachinformation (https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf); FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"Kaliumbromid DESITIN 850 mg Fachinformation (German national Fachinformation; https://www.desitin.de/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/Kaliumbromid-DESITIN-850-mg-Tabletten.pdf); PubMed PMID 31111774; PubMed PMID 22430156; FDA CVM KBroVet update (https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/cvm-updates/fda-grants-full-approval-drug-control-seizures-dogs-idiopathic-epilepsy); FDA Animal Drugs@FDA FOI Summary NADA 141-615 (https://animaldrugsatfda.fda.gov/adafda/app/search/public/document/downloadFoi/17867)","rationale":"DailyMed should not be used for the boxed-warning field and the AX powder label is an animal bulk ingredient, not a human antiseizure label. Add FDA veterinary and PubMed sources that support row status/